hi there steve kaufman here  another sunny morning in palm springs  i want to talk today about many stories  as a learning tool  remember if you enjoy these videos about  language learning please subscribe so  what are many stories many stories are  essentially what piotr  created over at  realpolis.pl stories which i used to  learn polish so they're not exactly  graded readers which is also a useful  sort of step towards authentic content  many stories are short you know five  eight ten line  stories  typically about a person doing something  in their daily life  the stories typically consist of three  parts  a description of  you know george gets up and reads the  newspaper  then george pours himself a cup of  coffee  simple stories like that and then the  second story mini story is the same  story  told by george i get up in the morning  so we have a repetition of the  vocabulary and then the third section  consists of questions did george get up  in the morning and of course  what's very good about what pyotr did  was he gives you the answer so you don't  have to rack your brain he says george  got up in the morning did george get up  in the morning  yes he got up in the morning or  did george sleep in  no george get up in the morning did  george sleep in no he didn't he got up  in the morning so  those are the three parts now you can  vary the formula somewhat uh and this is  what we have done  for these many stories that with  pyotr's permission we are creating in  english so that initially the in the  first we're going to do 100 of these the  first 20  the second section is a different person  so instead of she did this and this i do  this and this  then we start to introduce  different kinds of second stories where  we introduce we have the first story in  one tense and the second story in  another tense and again followed by  questions and as we progress we  introduce more and more concepts like  wish hope if art should  um  different aspects of the language and we  try as much as possible to bunch them  into different sections so that people  can go there not only when they're  learning the language but also to go and  practice some of the basic concepts and  and structures which continue to cause  trouble  now here's the pitch  i have had these translated into greek  for my own purposes and i'm studying  them on link  i love them i think it's very effective  um  i think it'd be it would be wonderful to  translate these into  many languages not only the languages  that we have at link of course we would  use them at link  for those languages that we're doing at  link but also other languages we don't  have farsi at link we don't have tagalog  we don't have indonesia  how many people out there  would be prepared to help out  to translate these stories  into other languages of course  immediate interest would be the stories  that we haven't linked  at least the languages we have at length  french chinese whatever arabic um so on  but also other languages and then we  make this available to people who are  learning languages whether they're doing  in that link or not  so we need translations and we need  recordings now the recording has to be  of a fairly good quality so does the  translation also the translation has to  be localized so if the english story  talks about mary and sam  then the spanish version has to talk  about i don't know consuelo and jorge  you can't and and then if there are  things that culturally don't fit they  have to be changed  also  while the some of the basic concepts  wish hope if ought to might they  translate into all different languages  but there will be specific language  issues in  certain languages and so we may have to  develop specific stories  for those issues  but always we want them translated into  all languages because of course at link  and elsewhere you know the idea that you  have a translation in your own language  is always helpful so that if you're  going through the story and you look up  the words and you've got the words and  you still can't make sense of it then at  least you can look at the translation of  that story so  long story short i would be interested  in knowing if there are people out there  who would be willing to get involved in  this project to develop these 100 mini  stories in as many languages as possible  uh to help the language learning world  of course we will use those  these stories at link  for those languages that we offer  but the stories themselves would be  public domain including the audio in  fact all of the audio and  text  lessons at link are available for free  download so it's only then people who  appreciate or value the functionality  that we offer link that would then  join link to study these stories but  other people can study them however they  want  so please let me know  and thank you for listening and how to  let me know  just email me  all right steve at link.com just email  me all right thanks for listening bye  for now  [Music]  you
 

